-- title:
--   Halving the shrinking radius leaves room for a ball
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a normed additive commutative group, let $S \subseteq E$, and let $\ell \in \mathbb{R}$. For $r \in \mathbb{R}$ define the *shrinking* of $S$ by $r$ as
--   $$S_r \;=\; \{\, y \in S \;:\; \bar B(y, r) \subseteq S \,\},$$
--   the set of points of $S$ having a full closed $r$-ball of room inside $S$ (applied to a double cone this is the paper's double half-cone $V_r$ of Definition 2.1). If $\zeta \in S_{2\ell}$, then
--
--   $$\bar B(\zeta, \ell) \;\subseteq\; S_{\ell}.$$
--
--   That is, a point with $2\ell$ of room to spare is surrounded by a ball of radius $\ell$ all of whose points still have $\ell$ of room. This is the self-improvement property of the shrinking operation, and it is the mechanism behind the two inclusions of Lemma 2.7: room at one scale can be traded for a neighbourhood of points each having room at half that scale.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No sign hypothesis is placed on $\ell$. For $\ell < 0$ the statement degenerates rather than fails: closed balls of negative radius are empty, so $S_{2\ell} = S_\ell = S$ and the inclusion is trivial. Only the normed group structure is used — no inner product and no completeness.
